{"connection_id": "BC-9429ffd1ed99ef51fab9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God chooses a longer route because the people might turn back", "note": "The commentary’s promise makes the way easier for its addressee; Exodus says God avoids the nearer Philistine route lest the people see war and return to Egypt, showing guidance adjusted to the travelers’ weakness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8548ef9ced82fe8f53b9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.13.21", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guide going before", "note": "YHWH goes before Israel in cloud and fire expressly to lead them on the road. This closely supplies the commentary's claim that the guide is named for advancing ahead of the travelers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3732292d42b30f6d1b21", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weak person held by two", "note": "The commentary gives the secondary image of a weakened person sustained between two people. Aaron and Hur support Moses' heavy hands from either side, supplying a concrete biblical pattern of two-sided aid to weakness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa61f17ed918914cf67f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.18.22", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "burden made easier", "note": "Moses' work is too heavy alone, and shared judgment will make it easier for him. This provides a narrative analogue to the commentary's ease as the removal of disabling strain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e66a1ec8eef0e97b0d92", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.20", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "messenger before the way", "note": "The promised messenger is sent before Israel to guard the way and bring them to a prepared place. It joins the commentary's forward guide, safe route, and arrival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-691ef728f567e7848ed2", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Moses turns aside to see the sign", "note": "The commentary treats turning aside from the reminder as the wretched movement. Moses says he will turn aside to see the burning bush, making the same spatial gesture an attentive response to a divine sign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aef40b28a0d9d5e48f8c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "God calls when Moses has turned aside", "note": "When YHWH sees Moses turn aside to look, God calls him by name. This completes the counter-image: a sideways movement that receives, rather than avoids, divine address."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da6d5ed4a107a2143bf7", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God promises to be with Moses’ mouth", "note": "The commentary cites Moses asking God to make his task easy; when Moses objects to speaking, God promises to be with his mouth and teach him what to say, a narrative answer that equips him for the mission."}
{"connection_id": "BC-966bd6d9498b86f4d93b", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.34.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the shepherd strengthens the sick and binds the injured", "note": "The commentary’s guide imagery includes a weak walker who relies on support; God as shepherd says he will bind the injured, strengthen the sick, and seek the lost, joining direction with care for the weak."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e08caf48c1c07adc6534", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.36.27", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Spirit causing obedient walking", "note": "God promises to put his spirit within Israel and cause them to walk in his statutes. It supplies a biblical model of divine enablement for the commentary's promise to make a person ready for the easy course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dafe400f3073cf643fed", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Abraham saddles a donkey for the journey", "note": "The commentary's rider-and-mount imagery ends by invoking Abraham's plea against idols. Genesis shows Abraham rising, saddling his donkey, and setting out, furnishing a literal Abrahamic journey without identifying its purpose with the Qur'anic prayer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-446d85546614b6c65b39", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.27", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "road and house", "note": "The commentary glosses h-d-y as showing someone both the road and the house. Abraham's servant says that God led him on the road to his master's kinsmen's house, giving the journey image a concrete destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7dfc6198db2b240c2e17", "ref": "WLC:Gen.24.61", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riding camels and following the guide", "note": "The commentary’s easy-to-follow animal carries a rider along a route; Rebekah and her attendants ride camels and follow Abraham’s servant, a concrete guided journey on mounts."}
