{"connection_id": "BC-cf788352168f8407dd9f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "possible Arabic s-w-y / Hebrew שוה cognate", "note": "The commentary reports an Arabic family sense of equality alongside “middle”; Isaiah uses the Hebrew root שוה for being equal or compared. The semantic link is specific, but the cognate relationship needs lexical confirmation and does not carry the camel-seat sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63fd2958e499db24cc92", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew shavah and the source label sawa", "note": "The commentary explicitly gives سوى the sense of equality and a balanced middle. Isaiah uses Hebrew שוה, a likely Semitic cognate, for being equal or comparable to the Holy One; its context checks the equality sense while supplying no camel-saddle image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-862795ba3d51c2e34661", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prepare a way in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary develops a guide who goes ahead and a route made easy; Isaiah’s voice calls for the Lord’s way to be prepared in the wilderness, an explicit road-making image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d296caf164cfda62c36d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "prepare YHWH's way in wilderness", "note": "The commentary treats guidance as showing a real road and making a journey possible. Isaiah's command to prepare YHWH's way and make a highway straight supplies the route-formula that precedes its leveling in 40:4."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2bc73c18635b4d3d1bb6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.31", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walk without growing faint", "note": "The commentary brings together weakness, light legs, and easy travel; Isaiah says those who wait for the Lord walk without fainting, portraying renewed capacity for the journey."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5fa9cf82e0ee02ade454", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountains lowered and rough ground made level", "note": "The commentary describes a long ridge that is nevertheless easier to climb; Isaiah pictures hills lowered and rough places leveled so a route can be traversed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21258f79c2f26da9953d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.4", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain and rough ground leveled", "note": "The commentary dwells on a long mountain ridge whose climb is made easier. Isaiah's preparation of the divine way lowers mountains, raises valleys, and smooths rough ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b262ce3cfbe4a0ac927d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass withers under divine breath", "note": "The pasture in the commentary is brought forth and then becomes dark waste. Isaiah's grass withers and flower fades when the breath of YHWH blows on it, a close vegetation-to-decay image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8a7885c005217c2fa2d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unknown paths made smooth", "note": "God leads blind people on unfamiliar roads and makes rough places level. This combines the commentary's guided traveler with its concern for a route made passable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-214917f126999f30056f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.43.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way in wilderness", "note": "God promises to make a way in wilderness and rivers in desert. This develops the commentary's divine route-making where the natural terrain itself offers no easy passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cad8a365ba8b565132ab", "ref": "WLC:Isa.45.18", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Creator forms and establishes the earth", "note": "The Creator forms, makes, and establishes the earth for habitation rather than emptiness. This enriches the commentary's brief claim that God creates and proportions, though it does not develop the travel imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0dc466cbba1efbd85b84", "ref": "WLC:Isa.48.17", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God teaches and leads the profitable way", "note": "YHWH teaches what benefits and leads Israel in the way it should go. It directly pairs the commentary's divine huda with yusr as a beneficial course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a16c675ae5012e251600", "ref": "WLC:Isa.5.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stubble, fire, rejected instruction", "note": "Isaiah makes dry grass and stubble burn because the people rejected YHWH's instruction. It binds the commentary's pasture becoming refuse to its adjacent scene of admonition refused and fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bbb0712f54fe96f8410", "ref": "WLC:Isa.52.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "not in haste or flight; God goes before", "note": "The commentary defines the guide as one who precedes and describes a calm walk rather than a flight; Isaiah joins both details, saying the departing people need not hurry or flee because God goes before them and guards their rear."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d19b037a5f695f4fa6a2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.52.12", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no haste or flight", "note": "The commentary contrasts an unhurried, composed walk with fleeing. Isaiah says the departing people will neither leave in haste nor flee because YHWH goes before them and guards behind."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c5b767b7cb28a20b88f4", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remove obstacles from my people's way", "note": "A voice orders a way built up and every obstruction removed from the people's path. This directly realizes the commentary's promise of a road made easy for its walker."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff28af9e3e50f5a0641b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.11", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "continual guidance and strengthened bones", "note": "YHWH promises continual guidance, satisfaction in dry places, and strength for the bones. Its linked guidance and bodily renewal answer the commentary's weak traveler and easy course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df8678e9045219f83acc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.58.14", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riding on the heights of earth", "note": "God promises to make the faithful ride on the heights of the earth. It gives a second prophetic use of riding and high terrain alongside the commentary's mount and mountain ridge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc094e6af6574ec24a44", "ref": "WLC:Isa.62.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "build highway and clear stones", "note": "The prophet tells people to pass through the gates, build up the highway, and clear its stones. Its public roadwork makes concrete the commentary's eased route rather than a merely inward disposition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-faec99b9990f2ab111dc", "ref": "WLC:Isa.63.13", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "horse without stumbling", "note": "God leads Israel through the depths like a horse in the wilderness so that it does not stumble. This directly links the commentary's guided animal and its light, sure movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16ebef4c7be8ee4d2a05", "ref": "WLC:Jer.21.8", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of life and way of death", "note": "YHWH sets before Jerusalem the way of life and the way of death. The formula supplies a direct prophetic counterpart to the commentary's one journey resolving into opposed ends."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3138eb4bb35332e23a42", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight path without stumbling", "note": "God leads the returning people by streams on a straight path where they will not stumble. This closely combines the commentary's guide, light movement, and secure arrival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-de6c0e70fbdb0ff5d540", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ancient paths, rest, and refusal to walk", "note": "The commentary says the wretched person leaves the reminder beside his route; Jeremiah tells the people to seek the good ancient path and walk in it, but they answer that they will not walk there, joining guidance, a way, and its refusal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e74a9fa88d614f6b8601", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good path offered and refused", "note": "YHWH tells the people to stand at the roads, ask for the good ancient path, walk it, and find rest; they answer that they will not walk in it. This is a close counterpart to the reminder that the wretched person avoids."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0326afe73651375f78ad", "ref": "WLC:Jer.7.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking backward after refusing to hear", "note": "Jeremiah says the people did not listen, walked in their own counsels, and went backward rather than forward. It makes the commentary's contrast between a forward guide and avoidance of admonition spatially explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a11adbc473b2aeca252", "ref": "WLC:Job.28.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weight for wind and measure for waters", "note": "Job describes God assigning weight to wind and measure to waters. This makes the commentary's divine measuring a material ordering of a world through which creatures travel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eaf5b0515fe68206b981", "ref": "WLC:Job.38.5", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "determined measurements of earth", "note": "God asks who fixed the earth's measures and stretched a line over it. It supplies a biblical cosmic instance of the measuring and proportioning named in the commentary on 87:2–3."}
{"connection_id": "BC-735291a8037e1921ba97", "ref": "WLC:Job.39.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives the horse its strength", "note": "God asks Job whether he gives the horse its strength and clothes its neck. It places the equine capacity behind the commentary's readily led horse within the Creator's ordering of creatures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a65663737b7badf47be1", "ref": "WLC:Jonah.1.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fleeing from YHWH's presence", "note": "Jonah physically sets out for Tarshish to flee from YHWH after receiving a commission. The narrative gives the commentary's avoidance of a divine reminder a full journey-shaped enactment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a915c496366d5a687bd8", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.2", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Terah served other gods", "note": "The commentary’s Abraham asks to be kept from idol worship; Joshua says Abraham’s father and ancestors served other gods, locating the danger his prayer names."}
{"connection_id": "BC-604082096a9c26b87faa", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "put away foreign gods", "note": "Joshua commands Israel to put away the foreign gods among them and incline the heart to YHWH. It makes the commentary's requested separation from idolatry an enacted turn of direction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4d1acba9dd5ef9256902", "ref": "WLC:Josh.24.3", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God took Abraham across the river", "note": "The commentary has Abraham ask God to keep his family from idols; Joshua recounts God taking Abraham from beyond the Euphrates into Canaan, a narrated removal from his idolatrous setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ecbfb8ba184a9b318d0", "ref": "WLC:Judg.5.10", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey riders, saddle-related sitting, and walkers on the road", "note": "The commentary brings together a riding cover, the mount, and a person’s route; Deborah’s song groups donkey-riders and walkers on the road with people sitting on a term sometimes understood as saddle-cloths, though that wording is uncertain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42ad697041af975ed012", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.19", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "arrogant and evildoers as stubble in fire", "note": "In Hebrew WLC versification, the coming day burns the arrogant and evildoers like stubble. It joins the commentary's vegetal residue, wretchedness, and final fire in one prophetic image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-15bda4852f5604a3f9ec", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "donkey turns aside from the blocked road", "note": "The commentary’s mount is easy to lead and quick to follow; Balaam’s donkey instead leaves the road when it sees the angel blocking the way, and that refusal protects its rider."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28066f29268661685749", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.23", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "donkey turns aside before the angel", "note": "The commentary reads the wretched person's tajannub as passing the warning by and keeping it at the roadside. Balaam's donkey turns aside from the road because it sees the armed divine messenger that its rider misses; here the sideways movement is the faithful response to warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63dadfe32c77918d800f", "ref": "WLC:Num.22.32", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Balaam’s way is perverse", "note": "The commentary makes the traveler’s conduct, not the road itself, decisive; the angel tells Balaam that his way is perverse, while the donkey’s apparent deviation is the safer response to the blocked path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ef57c2a97a3edd7ce98", "ref": "WLC:Prov.1.25", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Wisdom’s counsel refused", "note": "The commentary says the wretched person keeps the reminder at a distance; personified Wisdom says her hearers rejected her counsel and reproof, a concrete scene of refusing admonition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66afe2e76293b6525a80", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.24", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "upward path of life away from Sheol", "note": "For the prudent, the path of life leads upward in order to turn from Sheol below. It aligns the commentary's elevated road with its contrasting endpoint in the fire, though it does not share the animal imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f6e98e220a5b92f0261", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.9", "target": "sec14", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human plans, the Lord directs the steps", "note": "The commentary says the road can be the same while the traveler’s conduct differs; Proverbs contrasts a person’s plans with the Lord’s direction of his steps, clarifying the relation between human course and divine guidance."}

